WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.270
Two hundred years ago, the French
mathematician Joseph Fourier set

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:10.500
out to understand something quite modest:
how heat spreads through a bar of metal.

3
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:15.070
To solve the equation he wrote for it,
he broke the temperature into a sum

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.300
of simple, smooth waves.

5
00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:22.230
He could not have known that the same
idea would one day carry music,

6
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:26.170
compress photographs and let
doctors see inside the human body.

7
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.290
Equations often go this way.

8
00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:33.530
Someone writes one down to understand
a single thing, and it ends up running

9
00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:35.940
a part of the world they never imagined.

10
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.980
This video is about ten of them.

11
00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:44.250
The oldest is an idea about probability,
published in seventeen sixty three,

12
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.090
and the youngest is a formula

13
00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.670
for pricing financial contracts,
from nineteen seventy three.

14
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.450
Between them sit the laws of light

15
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.720
and magnetism, the shape of gravity,
and the wave that describes an atom.

16
00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:02.930
Then come the motion of air and water,
the slowing of moving clocks, the method

17
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:07.540
machines use to learn, and the limit
on how much any wire can carry.

18
00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:12.610
You won't need to follow the symbols,
because each equation is really a sentence

19
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:17.740
about how something changes, and we'll say
each of those sentences in plain words.

20
00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:22.090
They also lead into one another,
in a chain that runs from a beam

21
00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.620
of light all the way to the stock market.

22
00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.830
So we'll begin with light.

23
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.730
In the early eighteen hundreds,
electricity and magnetism looked

24
00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.940
like two separate curiosities.

25
00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:40.530
Then in eighteen twenty,
Hans Christian Ørsted saw a compass

26
00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.830
needle twitch beside a live wire,
and the two turned out to be connected.

27
00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:49.650
Around every charge and every
magnet there is a field, a push

28
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:54.710
or pull waiting at each point in space,
and iron filings let you see its shape.

29
00:01:55.750 --> 00:02:01.280
Forty years later, James Clerk Maxwell
gathered all of it into four equations.

30
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.750
Electric field lines spread outward
from charges, while magnetic field

31
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.950
lines never begin or end,
and always close into loops.

32
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.807
A changing magnetic field drives
the electric field around in a circle,

33
00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:17.710
which is how every power
station makes electricity.

34
00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.940
And a changing electric field does
the same to the magnetic field.

35
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.220
Those last two laws feed each other.

36
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.690
Each field, as it changes,

37
00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:32.740
creates the other, so together they can
travel through empty space as a wave.

38
00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.350
When Maxwell worked out its speed,
he got the speed of light.

39
00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:41.970
Light is an electromagnetic wave,
and so are radio,

40
00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.730
WiFi and every signal a phone sends.

41
00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:47.170
It seemed obvious that light

42
00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:52.050
travels in straight lines, until Einstein
asked what happens near something heavy.

43
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.610
Newton described gravity as a pull between

44
00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:01.070
masses, but in nineteen fifteen,
Einstein replaced that picture.

45
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.970
Mass bends space and time around it,
much as a heavy ball sinks

46
00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:10.950
into a stretched sheet, and anything
moving nearby simply follows the curve.

47
00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.110
The Earth orbits the Sun

48
00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:17.370
because that is the straightest path
open to it through curved spacetime.

49
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.530
The field equations put
this into a single line.

50
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.670
One side describes the shape of spacetime,

51
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.440
and the other the matter
and energy inside it.

52
00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.250
As the physicist John Wheeler put it,
matter tells spacetime how to curve,

53
00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.970
and spacetime tells matter how to move.

54
00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:41.430
Four years later, during a solar eclipse,
astronomers measured starlight bending

55
00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:44.840
around the Sun by just the amount
Einstein had predicted.

56
00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.150
Squeeze enough mass into a small enough

57
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:53.190
space, and the curve becomes so steep
that not even light can climb back out.

58
00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:55.930
That is a black hole.

59
00:03:56.950 --> 00:04:01.630
In twenty fifteen, the LIGO detectors
picked up ripples in spacetime from two

60
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.300
black holes merging, a stretch of less
than a hundredth of a proton's width.

61
00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:10.050
Einstein's equations
govern the very large,

62
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.790
but at the scale of a single atom, matter
stops behaving like a ball on a sheet.

63
00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:21.400
Fire electrons one at a time
at a barrier with two narrow slits,

64
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.160
and each one lands on the screen
as a single dot, like a tiny ball.

65
00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:30.950
But as thousands of dots build up,
they form stripes, and stripes like

66
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.810
that only appear when waves overlap.

67
00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:38.610
Each electron travels as a wave,
and lands as a particle.

68
00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.490
In nineteen twenty six,
Erwin Schrödinger wrote

69
00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.990
down the equation that governs this wave.

70
00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:46.970
It tells you how the wave

71
00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:50.330
changes from moment to moment,
given the energy around it.

72
00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.820
Where the wave is tall,
the electron is likely to be found.

73
00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.330
The wave can even leak through a wall

74
00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:01.100
that a ball could never cross,
an effect called tunnelling.

75
00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:04.730
Around a nucleus the wave is trapped,

76
00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:07.410
and it can only settle
into certain shapes.

77
00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:10.657
Those shapes are the orbitals of the atom,

78
00:05:10.707 --> 00:05:15.300
and they decide how atoms bond,
which is to say, all of chemistry.

79
00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:20.910
Lasers, computer chips and MRI scanners
were all designed with this equation.

80
00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:25.790
And a wave that spreads and swirls
as it moves turns out to describe

81
00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.050
something much larger than an atom.

82
00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:34.940
Pour cream into coffee, and it curls
into swirls that never quite repeat.

83
00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:39.250
To describe a flow like that,
think of the liquid as a huge number

84
00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:45.040
of tiny parcels, each one obeying Newton's
old rule that a push changes its speed.

85
00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:48.710
Claude-Louis Navier and George
Stokes wrote that rule

86
00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.160
out for fluids in the eighteen hundreds.

87
00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:55.580
Their equation adds up everything
that pushes on a parcel.

88
00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:00.160
The flow carries it along,
pressure shoves it from high to low,

89
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:01.600
and a kind of stickiness,

90
00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:05.470
called viscosity, drags it toward
the speed of its neighbours.

91
00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:08.300
Let water run past an obstacle,

92
00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:12.960
and the equation produces swirls that peel
away from one side and then the other.

93
00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:15.190
Weather forecasts,

94
00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:20.420
aircraft wings and models of blood flowing
through the heart are all built on it.

95
00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:23.750
Even so,
no one has proved that its solutions

96
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:27.510
always stay smooth in three dimensions,
and there is a million dollar

97
00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:29.360
prize for whoever settles it.

98
00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:33.710
Turbulence is a tangle
of swirls of every size,

99
00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:37.740
and to pull a tangle like that apart,
we need a different kind of tool.

100
00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:42.150
That tool is the one we met at the very

101
00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:46.000
start, from Joseph Fourier
and his bar of hot metal.

102
00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:50.370
Play three notes on a piano together,
and the sound reaching your ear

103
00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:54.590
is one bumpy wave,
yet you hear three separate notes.

104
00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:57.710
Fourier's claim was that any wiggle at all

105
00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:01.270
can be built by adding together simple,
smooth waves.

106
00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:05.310
The Fourier transform
runs this in reverse.

107
00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:09.880
It tests a signal against one
pure frequency after another,

108
00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.240
and when a test wave matches part
of the signal, the total grows large.

109
00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:16.280
What comes out is a list

110
00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:19.860
of the frequencies inside,
and how strong each one is.

111
00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:25.490
Even a drawing can be rebuilt this way,
from circles spinning at different speeds.

112
00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.650
Music files keep the strong frequencies

113
00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:33.700
and quietly drop the ones you won't miss,
photographs are compressed the same way,

114
00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:37.300
and MRI scanners build
their images from frequencies.

115
00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:42.570
Frequency is also how we keep time,
and the most precise clocks count

116
00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:45.020
the waves of light given off by atoms.

117
00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.330
So does every clock tick at the same rate?

118
00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:52.560
Imagine a clock made of light,

119
00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:57.460
a pulse bouncing between two mirrors,
where each bounce counts as one tick.

120
00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:01.730
Put that clock on a fast train,
and someone on the platform sees

121
00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:04.350
the light trace a longer, slanted path.

122
00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:08.650
In nineteen oh five, Einstein took
the speed of light to be the same

123
00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:13.810
for every observer, and a longer path
at the same speed takes more time.

124
00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.440
So the moving clock ticks more slowly.

125
00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:19.970
The triangle gives the exact amount.

126
00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:25.300
At everyday speeds the effect is far too
small to notice, but close to the speed

127
00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:27.730
of light it grows without limit.

128
00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:31.860
Muons,
particles created high in the atmosphere,

129
00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:35.610
should decay long before they reach
the ground, and they only make it

130
00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:37.620
because their clocks run slow.

131
00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.620
GPS depends on this.

132
00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:44.250
The satellites move fast,
so their clocks lose about

133
00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:46.640
seven millionths of a second a day.

134
00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:50.730
But they also sit higher in Earth's
gravity, where Einstein's field

135
00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:54.970
equations say time runs faster,
by about forty five millionths.

136
00:08:55.490 --> 00:08:59.010
Without correcting for both,
a GPS position would drift

137
00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:01.670
by more than ten kilometres every day.

138
00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:06.990
The phones that rely on those satellites
also predict the next word you will type,

139
00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:09.400
a skill learned by walking downhill.

140
00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:16.410
Suppose you are standing on a hillside
in thick fog, trying to reach the valley.

141
00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:18.460
You can't see the bottom,

142
00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:21.710
but you can feel which way
the ground slopes under your feet,

143
00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:25.320
so you take a small step downhill,
and then feel again.

144
00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:29.690
That is gradient descent,
and Augustin-Louis Cauchy

145
00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:32.150
described it in eighteen forty seven.

146
00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:35.500
The size of each step matters.

147
00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:38.840
Too small, and progress is painfully slow,

148
00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:42.100
and too large,
and you leap right past the valley.

149
00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:46.100
For a computer, the hill is its own error.

150
00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:49.870
Take a line that should fit some points,
and every step

151
00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:54.740
downhill turns and shifts it a little,
until the error is as small as it can be.

152
00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:59.820
A modern language model has billions
of these knobs instead of two,

153
00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:02.690
so its landscape has
billions of directions,

154
00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:05.320
but the rule stays exactly the same.

155
00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:10.740
Every answer such a model gives then
has to travel through cables and air,

156
00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:12.630
where signals pick up noise.

157
00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:16.360
So how much can a noisy
wire actually carry?

158
00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:22.647
In nineteen forty eight, Claude Shannon,
an engineer at Bell Labs,

159
00:10:22.673 --> 00:10:27.390
gave information a unit:
the bit, a single yes or no.

160
00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:33.760
A song, a photograph or a page
of text can all be written as bits.

161
00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:35.810
But any signal sent down a wire,

162
00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:38.770
or through the air,
collects noise along the way.

163
00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:44.850
To send more bits each second, you can
pack more signal levels closer together,

164
00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:48.190
but then the noise begins
to blur one into another.

165
00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:51.330
Shannon found the exact limit.

166
00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:53.370
It depends on how wide a band

167
00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:57.720
of frequencies you use, and how strong
the signal is compared to the noise.

168
00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:01.750
Under that limit,
errors can be made as rare as you like,

169
00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:03.940
and beyond it, nothing helps.

170
00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:08.890
Every WiFi and mobile network
is designed against this limit.

171
00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:11.570
Voyager One, more than twenty four

172
00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:16.790
billion kilometres away, still sends data
home with a radio of about twenty watts.

173
00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:20.930
The receiver hears a faint,
noisy signal and has to work

174
00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:22.980
out what was most likely sent,

175
00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:27.420
and reasoning backward from evidence like
that is the subject of our next equation.

176
00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:33.730
Suppose a disease affects one person
in a hundred, and a test catches

177
00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:38.600
nine in ten cases, but also flags
nine in a hundred healthy people.

178
00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:42.330
If you test positive,
how worried should you be?

179
00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:45.110
Think of a thousand people.

180
00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:48.870
Ten of them are ill,
and nine of those test positive.

181
00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:51.760
Of the nine hundred
and ninety who are healthy,

182
00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:54.290
about eighty nine test positive as well.

183
00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:59.710
So among all the positives,
only about one in eleven is really ill.

184
00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:04.760
Thomas Bayes, an English minister,
worked out how to reason this way,

185
00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:09.120
and his essay was published after
his death, in seventeen sixty three.

186
00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:12.720
His rule tells you how to update a belief.

187
00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:17.070
You start with how likely something
was before, weigh it by how well it

188
00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:18.760
explains the new evidence,

189
00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:22.430
and then compare that against every
way the evidence could have come about.

190
00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:26.900
Each new clue sharpens
the belief a little further.

191
00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:31.260
Spam filters work this way,
and so do self-driving cars.

192
00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:34.850
In twenty eleven,
searchers used it to locate the wreck

193
00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:39.520
of Air France flight four four seven,
within a week of starting a new search.

194
00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:44.440
And few things are as uncertain
as the future price of a share.

195
00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:48.220
Suppose you buy the right,

196
00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:53.270
but not the obligation, to buy a share
for one hundred dollars a year from now.

197
00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:57.610
If the price climbs to one fifty,
that right is worth fifty,

198
00:12:57.660 --> 00:13:00.050
and if it falls, you simply walk away.

199
00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:05.110
A contract like this is called an option,
and the question is what it

200
00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:08.640
should cost today,
when nobody knows where the price will go.

201
00:13:09.530 --> 00:13:12.120
In nineteen seventy three, Fischer Black,

202
00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:15.330
Myron Scholes and Robert
Merton found an answer.

203
00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:20.990
They treated the price as a random walk,
and then built a mix of the share and cash

204
00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:25.470
that moves exactly like the option,
so that the randomness cancels out.

205
00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:30.450
What remains is an equation
for the option's value as time runs out.

206
00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:35.030
With a change of variables,
it becomes the heat equation, the very one

207
00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:37.600
Fourier wrote for his bar of hot metal.

208
00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:43.110
It helped build a huge global market,
and it won Scholes and Merton a Nobel

209
00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:45.230
Prize in nineteen ninety seven.

210
00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:51.630
But it assumes that prices wander calmly,
and in real crashes they jump, which cost

211
00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:54.050
the traders who forgot it a great deal.

212
00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:58.760
An equation is a map of the world,
not the world itself.

213
00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:04.500
Looking back, each equation
handed something to the next.

214
00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:09.000
Light led us to gravity,
and gravity down to the atom.

215
00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:14.410
Waves led to water, water to frequencies,
and frequencies to clocks.

216
00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:18.430
Clocks led to learning machines,
learning to noisy wires,

217
00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.790
noise to evidence,
and evidence to markets.

218
00:14:22.810 --> 00:14:27.990
Ten equations, written over more than two
hundred years, mostly by people who simply

219
00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:30.070
wanted to understand one thing.

220
00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:35.210
Each one is a statement about how
something in the world changes.

221
00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:39.630
Like Fourier with his bar of metal,
the people who wrote them were usually

222
00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:43.070
trying to understand something small,
and what they found turned

223
00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:44.750
out to be much larger.

224
00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:46.980
Thanks for watching.
